1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,215
Have you ever noticed a pricing

2
00:00:02,215 --> 00:00:06,072
page with an overpriced hundred-forty-nine-dollar tier

3
00:00:06,072 --> 00:00:07,858
that almost nobody needs?

4
00:00:07,858 --> 00:00:09,858
You are not meant to buy it,

5
00:00:09,858 --> 00:00:12,430
you are meant to measure against it.

6
00:00:12,430 --> 00:00:15,146
Behavioral economist Dan Ariely tested this

7
00:00:15,146 --> 00:00:18,241
with Economist subscription offers in Predictably

8
00:00:18,241 --> 00:00:18,935
Irrational.

9
00:00:18,935 --> 00:00:20,830
With a print-only decoy at the

10
00:00:20,830 --> 00:00:22,662
same price as print plus web,

11
00:00:22,662 --> 00:00:25,251
eighty-four percent of students chose the

12
00:00:25,251 --> 00:00:26,325
expensive bundle.

13
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:27,462
Without the decoy,

14
00:00:27,462 --> 00:00:29,230
only thirty-two percent did.

15
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:31,760
Psychologists call this Price Anchoring.

16
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:34,986
Your brain struggles to calculate standalone value,

17
00:00:34,986 --> 00:00:36,884
so sellers plant a high anchor

18
00:00:36,884 --> 00:00:39,350
first to make seventy-nine dollars feel

19
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:39,730
cheap.

20
00:00:39,730 --> 00:00:42,941
Use the Blank-Sheet Test before any purchase:

21
00:00:42,941 --> 00:00:44,653
cover the pricing table,

22
00:00:44,653 --> 00:00:47,079
write down what solving that exact

23
00:00:47,079 --> 00:00:48,791
problem is worth to you,

24
00:00:48,791 --> 00:00:50,860
and reject anything above it.

25
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:53,300
A steep discount off a fake

26
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:55,830
anchor is still a real leak.
